---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import "../styles/site.css";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A single button — a gold circular cap set in a dark housing — encircled by concentric rings in different stroke styles, in flat two-ink risograph print"
  mainAttrs={{ "data-page": "home" }}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="hero-kicker">{courseMeta.code} · {courseMeta.session} {courseMeta.year}</p>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>How much interaction can one button contain?</h2>
  <p>
    Every player action in this course, all twelve weeks of it, comes from a
    single button — one bit of digital input, plus its timing. No second
    button, no stick, no keyboard shortcut standing in when things get hard.
    That's not a beginner constraint you graduate out of; it's the constraint
    the whole course tests.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Each week adds one new thing that single button can mean — starting with
    a plain press, then how long it's held, when it's released, the gap
    between presses, short-and-long sequences, a value that outlives the
    gesture, one press meaning different things depending on context, and on
    through movement, failure, a second player, and an input method built for
    someone who can't time a press themselves. Every week ends with a small
    prototype you build and test yourself, not a discussion. By week 12 you
    finish one complete one-button game, built by deciding which parts of
    that vocabulary it actually needs and cutting the rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone willing to build something playable most weeks, whether or not
    you've made a game before — week 1 assumes no prior prototypes. It suits
    you if you'd rather work inside a hard constraint and see what it forces
    open than start from a blank, unrestricted brief.
  </p>

  <div class="course-pathways">
    <h2>Start here</h2>
    <p>
      New to the course? <a href={withBase("/sessions/01-press/")}>Week 1: Press</a>{" "}
      is the first studio session, and its{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-01/")}>lecture</a> carries the opening
      slide deck. Nothing earlier is assumed.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <nav class="pathway-nav" aria-label="Course sections">
      <CardGrid columns={2}>
        <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
          <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
          <p>The framing behind each week's studio work, slide decks included.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
          <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
          <p>Prototype work, collaboration, accessibility, tools, and late work.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="People" href="/people/">
          <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
        </Card>
      </CardGrid>
    </nav>
  </div>
</ContentLayout>
